cff-version: 1.2.0
title: >-
  CleiMesh: signed, delegable permissions for AI agents, enforced by a
  WebAssembly sandbox and backed by replayable receipts
message: If you use this software, please cite it using these metadata.
type: software
authors:
  - given-names: Cleiton Augusto
    family-names: Correa Bezerra
    email: augusto.cleiton@gmail.com
    city: Porto Velho
    country: BR
doi: 10.5281/zenodo.23547249
repository-code: 'https://github.com/cleitonaugusto/cleimesh'
version: 0.1.1
date-released: '2026-10-21'
abstract: >-
  AI agents call tools and delegate work to other agents with all of their
  user's permissions. CleiMesh puts five pieces together. A permission is a
  signed token in the IETF Attenuating Agent Tokens JWT profile that can only
  narrow as it is delegated, offline. The permission becomes the configuration
  of the WebAssembly sandbox the tool runs in, where nothing is allowed by
  default. Every call, allowed or refused, leaves a signed, hash-chained
  receipt in the draft-sahu-agent-action-receipts format, and no call runs
  before its receipt is on disk. Calls run with a frozen clock and seeded
  randomness recorded in the receipt, so an auditor can replay a call and
  compare its output byte for byte instead of trusting the signer. Taint
  follows the delegation chain: once a tool that brings in outside content
  returns to an agent, tools that act on the world are refused for that
  agent's whole chain until a human clears it.
keywords:
  - AI agents
  - capability-based security
  - delegation
  - WebAssembly
  - sandboxing
  - prompt injection
  - audit
  - Model Context Protocol
  - Rust
license:
